Subject: Planning memo — six‑node transmission buildout with copper conductors and square‑law loss costs

We’ve been asked to design a new transmission network that connects two generator sites to four consumer load centers across a total of six nodes. Their map positions are provided in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and the role of each site (generation or load) is listed in {NODE_TYPES}. The consumer requirements are specified in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and the maximum available generation at each plant is given in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.

Design freedom and physical layout
- There are no predefined corridors. We may choose to build a line between any pair of nodes; if a line is not built, power cannot flow between those nodes.
- A line, once built, can carry power in either direction. However, each line has a thermal transfer rating and may not exceed the rating in either direction; the applicable rating is provided in {LINE_CAPACITY}.
- The cost to build a line is distance‑based. We will use the straight‑line separation between node coordinates, multiplied by the unit construction rate in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}.

Conductor and operating assumptions
- We will use copper conductors; the material property governing resistance is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the conductor cross‑section we intend to use is specified in {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. For a given span, resistance grows with length and falls with larger cross‑section, so long lines and smaller wires are penalized by higher resistance.
- The system operating level is {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}. This matters because, for a given power transfer, higher voltage yields lower current; since heating losses scale with the square of current, raising voltage sharply reduces losses (roughly with a 1/V² relationship).

Losses and how we value them
- Resistive losses follow a square law: pushing twice as much power through a given line produces about four times the heating losses. In physical terms, the loss on a line is proportional to its resistance and to (power/voltage)². This is a fundamental physics effect, not something we can safely approximate with a linear model.
- For planning purposes, we treat these losses as an explicit operating cost rather than a deduction from energy delivered. At the nodes, we will conserve power flow exactly: generators inject up to their limits, consumers receive at least their requirements (surplus delivery is acceptable), and the network balances injections and withdrawals without subtracting losses locally. The cost of the energy dissipated as heat is valued at the rate in {LOSS_COST_RATE}; effectively, each line’s heating translates into a recurring “heating cost” that grows with the square of the power we push through it.

Planning objective
- Our task is to decide which lines to build and how to route power so that every consumer’s demand is met while respecting generator limits and line ratings, and to do so at minimum total cost.
- Total cost combines two components: (1) capital costs driven by route length and the rate in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}, and (2) operational “heating costs” that reflect the square‑law dependence of resistive losses on flow, scaling with each line’s resistance and the operating voltage.
- Because losses grow nonlinearly, it is not sufficient to simply sum flows or apply a linear penalty. We need a planning model that captures the square‑law behavior explicitly. This will naturally favor higher‑voltage transfers, shorter routes, and (where appropriate) multiple paths that keep per‑line flows below the ranges where heating costs explode.

Data summary for the analysis
- Node positions: {NODE_COORDINATES}
- Node roles: {NODE_TYPES}
- Consumer requirements: {POWER_DEMANDS}
- Generator limits: {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}
- Conductor material and size: {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}
- Operating level: {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}
- Line rating: {LINE_CAPACITY}
- Construction cost rate: {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}
- Loss valuation: {LOSS_COST_RATE}

Deliverable
We will produce a network build plan—i.e., which candidate lines to construct—and an associated power routing strategy that meets all demands and generator limits while minimizing the sum of construction costs and square‑law heating costs. The result should make clear where higher voltage and shorter spans deliver outsized savings by cutting I²R losses, and where additional paths or capacity are justified to avoid the steep cost of pushing too much power through a single line.
